# npm user config for Netlify builds only, so the Hugeicons Pro license key
# reaches Netlify's "Install dependencies" stage.
#
# Netlify points npm here with the site environment variable
# NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG=/opt/build/repo/scripts/netlify.npmrc and supplies
# HUGEICONS_NPM_TOKEN. The laptop and the test server never read this file:
# without NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG, npm uses ~/.npmrc, which holds the real key.
# Putting this auth line in the committed .npmrc instead would override
# ~/.npmrc everywhere HUGEICONS_NPM_TOKEN is unset, and every install there
# would 401.
@hugeicons-pro:registry=https://npm.hugeicons.com/
//npm.hugeicons.com/:_authToken=${HUGEICONS_NPM_TOKEN}
